"Summarizes pairwise association coefficients in a symmetric matrix with a common bounded scale.\n\nUse when: Many numeric variables need a compact overview of pairwise association. The chosen coefficient matches the relationship type and measurement assumptions.\n\nAvoid when: Nonlinear patterns, groups, or unequal pairwise sample sizes would be hidden by one coefficient.\n\nVisual grammar: Row and column positions identify the variable pair. Cell color and optional printed number encode the correlation coefficient on a fixed −1 to +1 scale.\n\nBuild: Choose Pearson, rank-based, or another stated coefficient and missing-data policy. Compute pairwise coefficients and supporting observation counts. Draw a labeled symmetric or triangular matrix with a diverging color scale centered at zero.\n\nCheck: Check diagonal entries and symmetry are correct. Verify every coefficient lies within its valid range. Disclose pairwise sample sizes or use a consistent complete-case sample.\n\nPitfalls: Correlation is not causation and zero correlation does not guarantee independence. Inspect a scatterplot matrix before interpreting strong coefficients or apparent clusters.\n\nExample: Summarize synthetic vehicle measurements with Pearson correlations, a −1 to +1 legend, and pairwise sample counts available beside the display.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot matrix, Heatmap, Biplot."
